Per-package pkg-config directories for externals that live under /usr.

Spack adds <prefix>/lib/pkgconfig to PKG_CONFIG_PATH only for non-system prefixes, and
Spack's own pkgconf searches only its own prefix, so a configure script that asks
pkg-config for a /usr external fails (libjwt -> openssl). Each directory here holds
symlinks to the system .pc files of exactly one external, and the matching entry in
config/software.hpc/packages.external.yaml prepends that directory to PKG_CONFIG_PATH
through extra_attributes.environment. Linking only that package's files keeps the other
~200 system .pc files out of Spack builds.

Rules
- One directory per Spack package name; symlinks into /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkgconfig
  or /usr/share/pkgconfig, nothing copied, so Ubuntu updates flow through.
- The hook path is /cvmfs/hpc.ucdavis.edu/sw/spack-blue/config/pkgconfig/<name>, via the
  deployment's config symlink, so it does not depend on the worktree location.
- extra_attributes are part of the external's DAG hash. Adding or changing a hook gives the
  external a new identity and rebuilds its whole dependent closure; change hooks only when
  that rebuild is planned, and uninstall the stale external record afterwards.
- Spack applies the hook to direct build dependents of the external (BUILDTIME_DIRECT in
  build_environment.py); a link-only direct dependency does not receive it.
- An external with no .pc files on the system (apptainer) gets no directory and no hook.
- An external inside an installed compiler's link/run closure keeps its old identity in every
  solve until that compiler is rebuilt: the solver injects installed compilers and their
  closures into the reuse set regardless of concretizer:reuse (openssl via llvm -> python).
